name: xargs and doing it to everything
boss: Swarm
flavor: A thousand commands. One directive.
brief: Some commands read filenames as arguments; pipes carry text on stdin.
brief: xargs is the adaptor between them: it turns lines of input into
brief: arguments of a command.
brief:
brief: The classic trap is filenames with spaces. find -print0 emits names
brief: separated by a NUL byte, which cannot occur in a filename, and xargs -0
brief: reads them back. Use that pair by default and the problem never arises.
---
q: %find% every .log file and count the lines of each, using xargs
a: find . -name '*.log' | xargs wc -l
out: [0-9]
ref: xargs
teach: xargs
hint: xargs turns lines of stdin into arguments appended to the command
---
q: {do the same, safely, for filenames that might contain spaces|repeat that, but safe for filenames with spaces in them|redo it in a way that survives filenames containing spaces}
a: find . -name '*.log' -print0 | xargs -0 wc -l
out: [0-9]
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -0
hint: NUL is the one byte a filename cannot contain
---
q: {pass exactly one argument per command invocation|use xargs -n 1 to run the command once per input line|split the input so each invocation gets exactly one argument}
a: ls | xargs -n 1 echo
out: .
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -n
hint: -n caps how many arguments go into each invocation
---
q: {put each input where you want it, not just at the end, using a placeholder|use xargs -I to place each item wherever you want in the command|give xargs a placeholder so input can go mid-command, not just trailing}
a: ls *.log | xargs -I {} echo found {}
out: ^found .*\.log$
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -I
hint: -I defines a placeholder that gets replaced with each input item
---
q: {do nothing at all when the input is empty, rather than running once|when there's no input at all, run nothing rather than one empty invocation|make xargs skip running entirely on empty input}
a: printf '' | xargs -r echo nothing
why: primitive-cost
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -r
hint: without -r, an empty input still runs the command once with no arguments
---
q: {run at most 2 commands at the same time|use xargs -P 2 to run two invocations in parallel|cap concurrent xargs invocations at 2}
a: ls | xargs -P 2 -n 1 echo
out: .
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -P
hint: -P sets how many invocations run in parallel at once
---
q: {strip the directory part off the path @dir@/@txt@|use basename to drop everything up to the last slash in @dir@/@txt@|reduce @dir@/@txt@ down to just its filename}
a: basename @dir@/@txt@
out: ^@txt@$
ref: basename
teach: basename
hint: basename strips everything up through the last slash
---
q: {strip @txt@'s directory and its .txt suffix|use basename on @txt@ with a suffix argument to drop .txt too|reduce @txt@ down to its name with .txt also removed}
a: basename @txt@ .txt
out: .
ref: basename
teach: basename
hint: a second argument to basename is a suffix to strip as well
---
q: %show% the directory part of @dir@/@txt@
a: dirname @dir@/@txt@
out: ^@dir@$
ref: dirname
teach: dirname
hint: dirname is basename's mirror -- it keeps the directory and drops the name
---
q: {resolve @txt@ to its full absolute path|use realpath to turn @txt@ into an absolute path|find the absolute path realpath resolves @txt@ to}
a: realpath @txt@
out: ^/
ref: realpath
teach: realpath
hint: realpath resolves any relative pieces and symlinks along the way
---
q: {follow a symlink to whatever it finally points at|use readlink -f to chase @txt@'s link chain to the end|resolve @txt@ all the way through any symlinks}
a: readlink -f @txt@
out: ^/
ref: readlink
teach: readlink -f
hint: -f follows every link in the chain, not just the first one
---
q: {where does the command sed live|use which to find the path sed resolves to|ask which where sed lives on disk}
a: which sed
out: sed
ref: which
teach: which
hint: which searches PATH and reports the file it would run
---
q: {is sed a builtin, a file, or a function|ask type to classify what sed actually is|use type to find out what kind of command sed is}
a: type sed
out: sed
ref: type
teach: type
hint: type reports whether a name is a builtin, a file, a function or an alias
---
q: {remember where a command lives so the shell stops searching for it|use hash to cache sed's location for faster lookups|make the shell remember where sed is instead of searching PATH again}
a: hash sed
why: primitive-cost
ref: hash
teach: hash
hint: hash remembers a command's location so future lookups skip the PATH search
---
q: {check whether sed is available, the portable way|use command -v to check for sed portably, across shells|test whether sed exists using the POSIX-portable command -v}
a: command -v sed
out: sed
ref: command
teach: command -v
hint: -v prints the resolved command, or nothing if it isn't found
